WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.399
An offer is a one-page skeleton, not a brand deck.

00:00:03.399 --> 00:00:06.119
Fill it so someone can buy this week.

00:00:06.119 --> 00:00:08.159
This lesson is about offer skeleton.

00:00:08.159 --> 00:00:12.238
Who / problem / promise / process / price / next step.

00:00:12.238 --> 00:00:14.618
Do not treat this as theory only.

00:00:14.618 --> 00:00:18.017
Treat it as a move you can run this week.

00:00:18.017 --> 00:00:23.117
By the end, you should be able to act without waiting for a perfect plan.

00:00:23.117 --> 00:00:26.856
Keep the DigProg habit: watch, practice, then take one real action.

00:00:27.176 --> 00:00:31.118
Six boxes: who, problem, promise, process, price, and next step.

00:00:31.118 --> 00:00:33.483
Remember the objective: fill one-page offer.

00:00:33.483 --> 00:00:37.819
Write the idea in your own words before you move on.

00:00:37.819 --> 00:00:42.549
Name one concrete next action you can finish in under an hour.

00:00:42.549 --> 00:00:46.886
Avoid vanity work that feels busy but does not create proof.

00:00:46.886 --> 00:00:51.616
If something is unclear, simplify the promise and try a smaller version.

00:00:51.616 --> 00:00:54.375
Talk to a real person when possible.

00:00:54.375 --> 00:00:55.952
Feedback beats guessing alone.

00:00:56.272 --> 00:00:59.017
Fill the one-page offer in plain language.

00:00:59.017 --> 00:01:01.371
Empty boxes are homework, not mystery.

